        "1": {
          "evidence": "The horizontal-axis inscription reads 'Weekly testing volume (thousands)' and the vertical-axis inscription reads 'Positive cases detected'.",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "2": {
          "evidence": "The labeled purple marks progress generally from lower-left for Site A to upper-right for Site E; Site E is higher and farther right than Site D, and Site D is higher and farther right than Site C.",
          "status": "supported"
        }
      },
      "B": {
        "reading_checked": "If the horizontal axis encodes weekly testing volume and the vertical axis encodes positive cases, points positioned progressively farther right and higher indicate an increasing cross-site association between those plotted quantities. That pattern could justify advancing a network-wide claim only if the selected sentinel sites are accepted as sufficiently representative evidence for the network.",
        "evidence": "The axis labels are clearly printed and match the O assertions. The visual progression from Site A to Site E is clearly upward and to the right, indicating an increasing association between testing volume and positive cases. However, the B reading requires an unstated assumption: that the selected sentinel subset is representative of the network. This assumption is not supported by the chart. The chart explicitly limits the view to 'sites with the highest case loads', which is a selection bias. Therefore, while the pattern is visible, the B reading's necessary condition — that the subset is representative — is not met. The reading is refuted.",
